00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:20.040
Then came the 12th of Feb 2026, where&nbsp;
Apple dropped 5%. Basically wiping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:25.880
out all the gains Apple had for the year.
This was, you guessed it, due to Siri delays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:33.000
when a huge AI update was just weeks from launch.
People were even saying the Magnificent 7 should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.320
be changed to “Mag 6” to exclude Apple.
[Shannon Carroll, Quartz.com]:“Apple was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.000
late to the party, largely relied on&nbsp;
a partner rather than a breakthrough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.960
and has barely made a dent since. Rollout&nbsp;
has been sluggish, features are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.920
limited and easily found elsewhere, and&nbsp;
Apple hasn’t announced much else. In a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:56.440
market moment defined by fast, flashy AI&nbsp;
moves, the company has showed up late”&nbsp;

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:01.000
People were beginning to wonder…&nbsp;
was Apple past its prime?&nbsp;

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.800
Which, brings us to today, and it&nbsp;
seems like everything has done a 180.&nbsp;

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:14.040
Why is Apple now at the top again,&nbsp;
if they failed so spectacularly?&nbsp;

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:22.680
Apple somehow climbed back to #1.
They have since dropped down to #2, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:29.160
are still ahead of the hyperscalers, way ahead of&nbsp;
Alphabet and Microsoft, who are third and fourth.&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:34.120
Now, there is a counterargument here.
Some would say this is just because NVIDIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:40.280
dipped in July, which has now rebounded. That this&nbsp;
doesn’t really have anything to do with Apple.&nbsp;

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:46.760
But, this misses the bigger picture.
On the whole for 2026, Apple is up 24%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:50.600
against NVIDIA’s 4%.
Not only that, Apple is up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:57.160
nearly 60% over the trailing 12 months.
So, yeah it’s true NVIDIA dipped a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:02.840
but it doesn’t explain Apple’s resurgence.
Why are they still ahead of the hyperscalers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:07.000
with such little AI spend?
Or, did people only now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:12.680
realize that they were right all along?
To understand what’s really going on, we first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:16.920
need to look at maybe the most important benchmark&nbsp;
right now: Capex (show “capex” on screen).&nbsp;

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.880
Aka capital expenditure.
Funds companies spend to acquire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:30.040
upgrade, or maintain long-term physical assets.
Apple currently invest roughly 2.5% of their 2026&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:36.680
estimated sales in capital expenditure, or capex.
For the fiscal year 2025, ending September,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:42.680
Apple’s capex was: $12.7 billion.&nbsp;
With the AI‑related portion estimated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:47.720
around $11–12.7 billion.
The Fiscal 2026 projections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:55.320
are around $13–14 billion in AI‑related&nbsp;
capex, only a modest increase from 2025.&nbsp;

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:59.560
Sounds like a lot. I mean,&nbsp;
it’s $13 billion or more…&nbsp;

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.920
Until you look at the rest&nbsp;
of the magnificent seven.&nbsp;

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:11.240
Meta’s 2025 capex was $72 billion, with&nbsp;
guidance of $130 to $145B for 2026.&nbsp;

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:18.600
Alphabet was even higher, $80 billion&nbsp;
for 2025, $175 to $205B for 2026.&nbsp;

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:25.400
Microsoft’s was $64 billion in 2025,&nbsp;
estimated $190 billion in 2026.&nbsp;

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:31.240
And the biggest, by far, was Amazon.
$107B in 2025,&nbsp;

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:38.360
and an estimated $200 billion in 2026. Holy moly.
Most of that is towards AWS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:45.160
AI datacenters, and NVIDIA GPUs.
Apple’s capex is about 1/10th of Amazon’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:50.920
and less than 1/14th of their 2026 capex.
Most of the Mag 7 together are spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:57.080
about $700 billion, and on average are&nbsp;
putting 39% of sales into AI spend.&nbsp;

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:00.760
All except Apple.
Yet, this year they passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:07.560
all of these companies, and even NVIDIA.
So… how is it so low? And what’s changed?&nbsp;

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:12.840
Well, we already know part of the answer.
Instead of building their own model, licensing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:18.360
Gemini has cut down a huge amount of cost.
Paying a billion a year to license instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:23.240
of hundreds of billions to build out&nbsp;
your own? Pretty good idea it turns out.&nbsp;

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:28.360
And this is quite important, because&nbsp;
it allows Apple to do something else.&nbsp;

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:32.520
Apple has a very different focus for AI.
[Craig Federighi, at WWDC26] “At Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:38.280
we believe privacy in AI is non-negotiable.”
Apple actually first introduced this idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:45.400
back at WWDC24, when they first introduced&nbsp;
*Private Cloud Compute.* More on that later.&nbsp;

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:51.800
Apple’s core bet right now, is that the most&nbsp;
useful AI should run locally. On your iPhone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:58.680
iPad, Mac, not in a remote data centre.
Local AIs are fast, more cost-effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:03.080
and importantly: Private. (show headline).
But, it can’t realistically run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:05.720
everything locally.
For heavier tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:12.760
Apple Intelligence uses “Private Cloud Compute.”
Apple does own data centres, and it’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:16.680
owned them for over a decade, but&nbsp;
for iCloud services, Apple Maps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:22.760
the App Store, among other things.
Data centres aren’t just for AI, remember.&nbsp;

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:27.960
So, they refitted some of them for Private Cloud&nbsp;
Compute, for when Apple customers need cloud-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.880
AI, which Apple charges for.
But not all of it.&nbsp;

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:36.840
For the heaviest workloads, Apple doesn’t&nbsp;
really use any of its own data centres.&nbsp;

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:41.800
They also don’t build out massive data&nbsp;
centres like the other hyperscalers.&nbsp;

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:47.000
Instead, they lease compute and cloud&nbsp;
capacity from Google, using Google Cloud.&nbsp;

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:54.440
Apple isn’t ignoring AI. Well, in some ways it is.
It’s running AI in a different business model:&nbsp;

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:59.400
On‑device first, cloud second, and&nbsp;
almost no owned datacenter empire.&nbsp;

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:05.480
Apple wisely runs AI where it can on-device, in&nbsp;
their existing infrastructure, but beyond that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:08.120
instead of spending hundreds&nbsp;
of billions building out more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:13.240
data centres, it just rents them.
Okay, so why does the market care?&nbsp;

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:18.040
Apple isn’t spending money,&nbsp;
and is renting AI. So what?&nbsp;

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:28.280
Well, because they control&nbsp;
something more valuable.&nbsp;

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:31.880
If you’ve watched this far, consider&nbsp;
subscribing! It helps the channel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:36.680
means you won’t miss future videos.
I think everyone realized that Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:42.600
despite not spending so much on AI, still&nbsp;
owns one of the most valuable layers of AI.&nbsp;

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:45.640
The layer where the users are.
[Nicolas Cote-Colisson, HSBC]:“Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:50.840
is now at an operational turning point: not&nbsp;
only can the company stay away from the (too)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:56.840
high capex debate… it is also well placed to&nbsp;
leverage its 2.5 billion installed device base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:01.000
with its forthcoming revamped Apple Intelligence.”
[Toni Meadows, BRI Wealth Management]: *“Apple is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:05.960
less exposed to capex intensity and better&nbsp;
positioned to monetize AI via services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:11.320
ecosystem lock-in, and hardware upgrades.”*
The economics of this are pretty smart.&nbsp;

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:17.080
OpenAI, for example, has around&nbsp;
900-910 million weekly active users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:24.280
95% of them are on the free service.
So they have a -122% operating margin.&nbsp;

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:31.400
But Apple has 2.5 billion active devices.&nbsp;
Even if they all use Apple Intelligence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:37.640
Apple's marginal cost per AI prompt is&nbsp;
basically zero. Because it’s running locally.&nbsp;

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:42.200
The silicon, the electricity and the&nbsp;
depreciation all belong to the customer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:47.720
who already paid for the iPhone, iPad, etc.
This also cuts the need for them to own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:52.840
giant amounts of GPU clusters, which can&nbsp;
lose half their value in eighteen months.&nbsp;

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:58.040
But this local AI use is limited,&nbsp;
though this isn’t a bad thing.&nbsp;

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:02.840
Apple Intelligence does carry daily&nbsp;
usage limits, like any other LLM.&nbsp;

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:09.160
iCloud+ subscribers get even more AI use.
But that’s just another way to monetize.&nbsp;

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:14.920
When it starts to cost Apple, aka during&nbsp;
*Private Cloud Compute,* is when they charge.&nbsp;

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:19.480
Then, when users want the most costly AI&nbsp;
use, they just license it from Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:24.200
and charge the user, passing on the cost to&nbsp;
them, while making a little bit of money.&nbsp;

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:29.640
So Apple wins in basically every case.
Unlike other AI companies, who lose money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.480
on free users, and make a little&nbsp;
bit on users who want more AI use.&nbsp;

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.640
“While Apple was initially bruised by&nbsp;
many investors for not joining the LLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:42.920
investment cycle, investors are coming around&nbsp;
to Apple's industry-leading [free cash flow]”.&nbsp;

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:49.560
The AI moat isn’t really how many GPUs or data&nbsp;
centres it owns, but all the devices it controls.&nbsp;

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:52.040
2.5 billion.
Which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:57.240
spend is low, but cash flow is still high.
This means they can return good money to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:02.680
investors, which is becoming less common nowadays.
That explains why they’ve returned to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:08.200
top. Well, they were at the top.
Ironically, this isn’t anything new.&nbsp;

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:11.880
They never wanted to own all the&nbsp;
infrastructure, they wanted to own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:17.560
the layer with all the margins and behaviour.&nbsp;
And this has basically always been the case.&nbsp;

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:22.920
But, strangely, Apple predicted&nbsp;
the future of AI: Commoditization.&nbsp;

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:29.000
Apple does not need to build the best model. It&nbsp;
only needs multiple companies competing to supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:36.920
one. OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, Meta and others&nbsp;
continue racing, model quality rises, prices fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:42.920
supply expands, intelligence commoditizes.
The entire AI industry is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:47.787
going through a massive shift.
This is the exact reason OpenAI is struggling.&nbsp;

00:14:47.787 --> 00:14:52.760
[Josh Rowe]: “While Apple didn’t build any AI&nbsp;
infrastructure, they own one of the most valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:57.400
layers. OpenAI, despite committing&nbsp;
to over a trillion in AI spend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.400 --> 00:15:03.240
don’t actually own a valuable part of the AI&nbsp;
ecosystem, and perhaps the least valuable.”&nbsp;

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:07.400
It turns out, while Apple controls&nbsp;
the end users, OpenAI controls a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:13.080
layer which is becoming less and less valuable.
Yes, they’ve still committed to spending so much.&nbsp;

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:16.120
Click this video to learn&nbsp;
the other side of the story.

